 1 file changed, 448 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 scripts/tracing/README.md

diff --git a/scripts/tracing/README.md b/scripts/tracing/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..e2315a37161c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scripts/tracing/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,448 @@
+# Tracefs Object Watch
+
+This directory contains a small, profile-based tracing framework built on top of
+`tracefs` dynamic events. Its goal is to trace object lifecycles and bug-specific
+events without adding new kernel instrumentation for every debugging task.
+
+The main entrypoint is `scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh`.
+
+## What This Framework Is For
+
+Use this framework when you want to:
+
+- trace allocation and free activity for one object family
+- limit tracing to one target process tree
+- integrate tracing with a proof-of-concept (POC) in C
+- keep setup noise out of the trace by enabling tracing only around the bug window
+- add new object watchers without rewriting the whole tracing script
+
+Use this framework before adding ad hoc kernel `printk()` hooks.
+
+## Directory Layout
+
+```text
+scripts/tracing/
+  tracefs-object-watch.sh          # runner / wrapper / lifecycle management
+  README.md                        # this document
+  object-watch/
+    lib.sh                         # shared helpers
+    poc_control.h                  # C helper for begin/end/mark control
+    profiles/
+      cache.sh                     # generic cache-backed watcher
+      request_sock.sh              # request_sock specialization
+```
+
+## High-Level Design
+
+There are two layers:
+
+1. Core runner: `tracefs-object-watch.sh`
+2. Profiles: `object-watch/profiles/*.sh`
+
+The runner knows how to:
+
+- create and clean up a tracefs instance
+- register dynamic events
+- enable and filter those events
+- run a target program under tracing
+- capture descendant tasks in wrapper mode
+- receive begin/end/mark commands from the POC
+
+A profile knows:
+
+- which events define the object lifecycle
+- which fields should be fetched from those events
+- which filters should be applied
+- which object-specific fallbacks are acceptable
+
+This split is important. Keep the runner generic. Put object-specific logic in
+profiles.
+
+## Current Profiles
+
+### `cache`
+
+Generic watcher for cache-backed objects using the built-in `kmem` tracepoints.
+
+It traces:
+
+- `kmem_cache_alloc`
+- `kmem_cache_free`
+
+It requires BTF because it fetches `s->name` to identify the cache name.
+
+Typical use:
+
+```bash
+sudo scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh start \
+  --profile cache \
+  --pid 1234 \
+  --cache 'cred_jar'
+```
+
+### `request_sock`
+
+Specialization for `request_sock` debugging.
+
+It builds on the generic cache watcher and adds wrapper probes such as:
+
+- `inet_reqsk_alloc`
+- `cookie_tcp_reqsk_alloc`
+- `inet_reqsk_clone` when available
+
+Important design detail:
+
+- free tracking does **not** depend on `__reqsk_free()`
+- instead, free tracking falls back to `kmem_cache_free` filtered by
+  `request_sock*`
+
+This is the correct shape for the framework. `__reqsk_free()` is a special case,
+not a core framework abstraction.
+
+## Common Commands
+
+### Static start/stop mode
+
+Use this when you already know the target PID and do not need wrapper mode.
+
+```bash
+sudo scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh start \
+  --profile request_sock \
+  --pid 1234
+
+sudo cat /sys/kernel/tracing/instances/objtrace/trace_pipe
+
+sudo scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh stop
+```
+
+### Wrapper mode
+
+Use this when the framework should launch the POC itself.
+
+```bash
+sudo scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh run \
+  --profile request_sock \
+  -- ./fast_poc
+```
+
+This is the preferred mode for exploit debugging because it avoids manual PID
+handling and supports descendant capture.
+
+### Discoverability
+
+```bash
+scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh --help
+scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh start --list-profiles
+scripts/tracing/tracefs-object-watch.sh start --profile request_sock --profile-help
+```
+
+## Wrapper Mode Semantics
+
+Wrapper mode exists because a plain tracefs setup cannot automatically know which
+PID you care about. The runner solves that by launching the POC itself.
+
+The flow is:
+
+1. Create tracefs instance
+2. Install profile events and filters
+3. Spawn the target child in a stopped state
+4. Enroll the child into `set_event_pid`
+5. Enable `event-fork`
+6. Resume the child
+7. Let the POC toggle tracing with a FIFO control channel
+
+### Threads and child processes
+
+In `run` mode, the runner does **not** use `common_pid == X`.
+
+Instead it uses:
+
+- `set_event_pid`
+- `event-fork`
+
+This means the trace follows the wrapped task tree:
+
+- the wrapped process
+- its threads
+- forked child processes
+- further descendants
+
+This is usually what you want for a multi-threaded or multi-process POC.
+
+This does **not** capture unrelated pre-existing processes.
+
+## POC Integration
+
+Wrapper mode exports a FIFO path in the environment variable:
+
+```text
+TRACEFS_OBJWATCH_CTL
+```
+
+The POC can use `object-watch/poc_control.h` to send control commands to the
+runner without writing to tracefs itself.
+
+Supported commands are:
+
+- `begin [tag]`
+- `end [tag]`
+- `mark <text>`
+- `clear`
+
+### Recommended bug-window pattern
+
+Most POCs have noisy setup that is unrelated to the actual trigger. The intended
+pattern is:
+
+1. run all setup logic with tracing disabled
+2. call `objwatch_begin()` immediately before the interesting path
+3. optionally call `objwatch_mark()` around key milestones
+4. call `objwatch_end()` when the bug window is over
+
+Example:
+
+```c
+#include "scripts/tracing/object-watch/poc_control.h"
+
+static void do_setup(void)
+{
+	/* setup noise */
+}
+
+static void trigger_bug(void)
+{
+	objwatch_begin("trigger");
+	objwatch_mark("about to send racing packet");
+	/* actual bug trigger */
+	objwatch_end("trigger");
+}
+
+int main(void)
+{
+	do_setup();
+	trigger_bug();
+	return 0;
+}
+```
+
+Current behavior:
+
+- `begin` clears the trace buffer, writes a marker, then enables tracing
+- `end` writes a marker, then disables tracing
+- `mark` adds a trace marker only
+
+If the POC is not running under wrapper mode, the helper returns `-ENODEV`.
+
+## Rules For Future LLMs
+
+If you are extending this framework, follow these rules:
+
+1. Do not put object-specific logic in `tracefs-object-watch.sh` unless it is
+   truly generic runner behavior.
+2. Prefer adding a new profile in `object-watch/profiles/`.
+3. Prefer built-in tracepoints first, then fprobes, then kprobes. Only add new
+   kernel tracepoints if tracefs cannot express the required event precisely.
+4. Do not assume inline helpers are probeable. Check whether the target is:
+   - out-of-line exported/global symbol
+   - static symbol with build-dependent visibility
+   - inline helper with no symbol at all
+5. If an exact free routine is not probeable, use a semantic fallback in the
+   profile instead of polluting the framework core.
+6. Keep profile fallbacks explicit in comments and README updates.
+7. Update this README when you add or change profiles.
+
+## How To Add A New Object Watcher
+
+### Step 1: Decide whether the object is cache-backed
+
+If the object lives in a dedicated `kmem_cache`, start from the `cache` profile
+pattern.
+
+Examples:
+
+- object allocated from `kmem_cache_alloc(cachep, ...)`
+- object freed by `kmem_cache_free(cachep, obj)`
+
+If the object is created by a higher-level wrapper, you can still use cache
+events for alloc/free and add function probes for extra semantics.
+
+### Step 2: Find candidate events
+
+Look for candidates in this order:
+
+1. existing tracepoints
+2. function entry/exit probes on stable wrappers
+3. kprobe/kretprobe on lower-level functions
+4. only if necessary: add a new kernel tracepoint
+
+Prefer events that already carry:
+
+- object pointer
+- cache pointer or cache name
+- size / flags / node
+- wrapper arguments useful for correlation
+
+### Step 3: Identify lifecycle boundaries
+
+For a new profile, define:
+
+- alloc events
+- free events
+- optional clone/migrate events
+- optional context-only events
+
+Be honest about what is exact and what is fallback-based.
+
+Example for `request_sock`:
+
+- alloc: exact wrapper return probes
+- free: fallback to `kmem_cache_free` on `request_sock*`
+
+### Step 4: Create a new profile file
+
+Add `scripts/tracing/object-watch/profiles/<name>.sh`.
+
+Each profile must define:
+
+- `profile_init`
+- `profile_usage`
+- `profile_parse_args`
+- `profile_validate`
+- `profile_install`
+- `profile_apply_filters`
+- `profile_summary`
+
+Use the helper functions from `object-watch/lib.sh` rather than directly writing
+to tracefs from the profile.
+
+### Step 5: Install events with the shared helpers
+
+Useful helpers from `lib.sh`:
+
+- `register_event <name> <dynamic-event-definition>`
+- `try_register_event <name> <dynamic-event-definition>`
+- `set_event_filter <event> <expr>`
+- `install_cache_tracepoint_events`
+- `apply_cache_tracepoint_filters`
+- `compose_pid_filter`
+- `compose_cache_filter`
+- `append_filter_clause`
+
+Use `try_register_event()` for symbols that may not exist in every build.
+
+### Step 6: Validate assumptions
+
+Before finalizing a profile, check:
+
+- whether BTF is required
+- whether the symbol is static or inline
+- whether the fetched fields are valid for the chosen event
+- whether wrapper mode or manual PID mode is the intended usage
+- whether multi-thread or multi-process descendants should be captured
+
+### Step 7: Document the profile
+
+At minimum:
+
+- add profile help text
+- update `list_profiles` in the runner
+- update this README
+- mention exact vs fallback lifecycle boundaries
+
+## Profile Template
+
+Use this as a starting point:
+
+```bash
+#!/bin/bash
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+profile_init() {
+	if [[ -z "${TARGET_CACHE_GLOB:-}" ]]; then
+		TARGET_CACHE_GLOB='my_cache*'
+	fi
+}
+
+profile_usage() {
+	cat <<'EOF'
+Profile: my_object
+
+Purpose:
+  Trace my_object lifecycle.
+EOF
+}
+
+profile_parse_args() {
+	if [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; then
+		die "profile my_object does not accept extra options: $*"
+	fi
+}
+
+profile_validate() {
+	require_btf_or_die
+}
+
+profile_install() {
+	install_cache_tracepoint_events
+
+	register_event my_wrapper_entry \
+		"f:$GROUP/my_wrapper_entry my_alloc_wrapper arg1=foo"
+	register_event my_wrapper_exit \
+		"f:$GROUP/my_wrapper_exit my_alloc_wrapper%return obj=\$retval"
+}
+
+profile_apply_filters() {
+	local pid_filter
+
+	apply_cache_tracepoint_filters
+	pid_filter=$(compose_pid_filter)
+	set_event_filter my_wrapper_entry "$pid_filter"
+	set_event_filter my_wrapper_exit "$pid_filter"
+}
+
+profile_summary() {
+	log "cache filter: $TARGET_CACHE_GLOB"
+}
+```
+
+## Common Pitfalls
+
+### Inline helpers
+
+Do not design around inline helpers unless you are intentionally adding a real
+kernel tracepoint somewhere else.
+
+If the code lives in a header as `static inline`, it is usually the wrong probe
+anchor.
+
+### Static symbols
+
+A static function may or may not be probeable depending on the build and symbol
+visibility. Use `try_register_event()` for these.
+
+### BTF dependency
+
+This framework currently expects BTF for the useful profiles because it fetches
+fields such as `s->name` or `ops->slab_name`.
+
+Without BTF:
+
+- cache-name decoding will fail
+- many field-based fetch expressions will fail
+
+### Overfitting one bug
+
+Do not hardcode one bug's special cleanup path into the framework core.
+
+The framework should stay reusable. Put one-off semantics in profiles.
+
+## When To Add A Real Kernel Tracepoint
+
+Add a new kernel tracepoint only when all of the following are true:
+
+- tracefs dynamic events cannot observe the required lifecycle event precisely
+- a wrapper or fallback event loses important semantics
+- userspace correlation becomes unreliable or too fragile
+- the event is likely to be reused beyond one temporary debugging session
+
+Until then, prefer this framework.
-- 
